edb/ref 17592186054544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054628 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0e8b25b3-5cc4-51b7-8ff9-fc1773797ad4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054889 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054889 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054889 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054889 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054889 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054889 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054889 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054889 #edb/ref 1045 "The Arithmetic of Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054889 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186055528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054889 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054889 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "14506510-6a23-50e7-9f92-2d6a0e7b9372" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055528 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055528 #edb/ref 1061 13680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055528 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "10d1d758-8351-5c00-a262-8b57b04f8398" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055528 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186055964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055529 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055529 #edb/ref 1061 13682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055529 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "725779d7-8771-596d-841c-0ca2dd5da761" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055529 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055530 #edb/ref 1061 13683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055530 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c6da4ec9-5a2e-5037-ad6d-2b91d02a4fa0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055530 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055531 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055531 #edb/ref 1061 13684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055531 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "722ef25c-0cfd-5916-9719-a70b3e79029b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055531 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055551 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055551 #edb/ref 1061 15087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055551 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d9de7d87-ac26-5915-a421-2a3c6f706e14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055551 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055552 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055552 #edb/ref 1061 15088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055552 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c0ba47b0-a276-56f7-965d-acc1c060fc97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055552 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186055971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055964 #edb/ref 1064 "It's possible to perform arithmetic with functions, just like with numbers. Shown below are the four arithmetic operations, addition $(+)$, subtraction $(-)$, multiplication $(\\cdot)$, and division $(/)$, applied to the functions\n\n$$\nf(x) = 4x + 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ng(x) = x - 2\n$$\n\n- **Addition**\n$\\begin{aligned} (f + g)(x) &= f(x) + g(x) \\\\ &= (4x + 1) + (x - 2) \\\\ &= 4x + 1 + x - 2 \\\\ &= 5x - 1 \\end{aligned}$\n- **Subtraction**\n$(f - g)(x)|= f(x) - g(x); = (4x + 1) - (x - 2); = 4x + 1 - x + 2; = 3x + 3$\n- **Multiplication**\n$(fg)(x)|= f(x)g(x); = (4x + 1)(x - 2); = 4x^{2} - 8x + x - 2; = 4x^{2} - 7x - 2$\n- **Division**\n$(f/g)(x)|= (f(x))/(g(x)); = \\frac{4x + 1}{x - 2}$\n\n**Note:** In the case of division, we must have that\n\n$$\ng(x)\\neq 0\n$$\n\nso that we avoid dividing by zero." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055964 #edb/ref 1065 2751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055964 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c656d962-19ad-5e04-b117-fa8d0c9034b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055964 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055971 #edb/ref 1064 "When we're asked to evaluate the sum of two functions at a particular point, there are two methods that we can use. We could either evaluate both functions at the point and sum the results. Or, we could sum the two functions and then evaluate the result at the point.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the $(f+g)(1)$ for\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^2 -1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ng(x) = 2x-3\n$$\n\nThe notation $(f+g)(x)$ means the sum of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(f + g)(1)$, we find $f(1)$ and $g(1)$, and then add the results:\n\nFirst, we find $f(1)$ and $g(1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(1) &= (1)^{2} - 1 = 0 \\\\\ng(1) &= 2(1) - 3 =-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we add the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f + g)(1) &= f(1) + g(1) \\\\\n&= 0 + (-1) \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(f+g)(x)$ by adding the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f + g)(x) &= (x^{2} - 1) + (2x - 3) \\\\\n&= x^{2} + 2x - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(f + g)(1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f + g)(1) &= (1)^{2} + 2(1) - 4 =-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth these methods work fine, but method $1$ is usually faster.\n\nFinally, note that we can evaluate the difference between two functions using the same methods." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055971 #edb/ref 1065 2834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055971 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "74671b66-6f9b-59a5-96e2-aeb7ca503afe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055971 #edb/ref 1067 "Evaluating the Sum or Difference of Two Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
